= 26 August 2024 — The emergency exception becomes a business model

Prosecutors examining RARS say they are investigating suspected preferential
treatment, excessive prices and use of special reserve rules to bypass normal
procurement. One cited generator chain bought equipment in China for roughly
**PLN 69 million** and sold it to RARS for more than **PLN 350 million**. The
inquiry covers generators, masks and other emergency goods linked in reporting
to businesses around the patriotic-clothing label **Red is Bad**.

On 22 August prosecutors prepare organised-group and abuse-of-authority charges
against former RARS chief Michał Kuczmierowski and businessman Paweł Szopa.
Because the former chief's whereabouts are unknown and the allegations have not
been formally read to him, a wanted notice follows on 26 August. These are
charges and suspicions in an active case, not verdicts.

[? if emergency_procurement_standard = 1: In 2020 we answered the ventilator
contract by writing a seven-day counterparty, authorisation and delivery rule.
RARS can now be measured against the standard we already published.?]
[? if procurement_scandal_stance = "Audit referral": The ventilator file
taught us to preserve transfers and signatories for auditors. Four years
later, the reserve agency has supplied the larger sequel.?]
[? if procurement_scandal_stance = "Ward-level campaign": Our old campaign
began with the public service that never received the goods. The reserve case
offers firefighters, hospitals and Ukraine the same material route.?]
[? if procurement_scandal_stance = "Corruption attack": We used the arms
dealer as a complete sentence in 2020. Red is Bad gives television a sequel
and gives us no institutional answer we prepared earlier.?]

- @rars_ledger
- @rars_case
- @rars_merch

