= 12 August 2026 — The return address is the finding

Marcin Romanowski, former deputy justice minister and a suspect in the Justice
Fund case, sends the Warsaw Regional Court a personally signed application for
safe conduct. He promises to appear if he may remain free while proceedings
continue. The address written on the application is in **Tyraspol**, capital of
the Russian-backed separatist territory of Transnistria. The court confirms
that the application arrived on 4 August; prosecutors receive a copy.

This is stronger than an anonymous airport photograph and weaker than an
arrest. It is Romanowski's own declared address in a court document. It also
turns the right's argument about European political asylum into something
stranger: after Hungary withdrew his refugee status, a former minister accused
over a fund for crime victims asks for the protection of Polish procedure from
a territory whose institutions Poland does not recognise as a state.

[? if ziobro_whereabouts_stage <= 2: The Ziobro whereabouts desk has barely
opened and already needs a second name, another coloured line and a map inset
large enough to explain why Moldova does not control the destination.?][? if
ziobro_whereabouts_stage > 2: Ziobro's itinerary is already national
entertainment. Romanowski has now supplied the first destination on either map
with his own handwriting and a case number.?]

- @romanowski_transnistria_legal
- @romanowski_transnistria_conduct
- @romanowski_transnistria_show

@romanowski_transnistria_legal
title: Use the signed address and work through Moldova and European partners
subtitle: Treat Tyraspol as evidence and a jurisdictional problem, not as permission for a fantasy extraction operation.
on-arrival: foreign_policy_capacity += 4; prosecution_oversight_capacity += 4; institutional_trust += 6; public_trust += 4; judicial_legitimacy += 3; left_poll_momentum += 0.25; pis_poll_momentum -= 0.15; public_mood_pending_rule_of_law_left_ownership += 6; public_mood_pending_national_security_support += 2; public_mood_pending_national_security_salience += 4; news_headline = "Warsaw turns Romanowski's Tyraspol address into a legal-cooperation file"

The address is verified, preserved as evidence and sent through Moldova's
recognised authorities with requests to partners who can trace travel and
communications. Nobody promises that a Polish warrant has suddenly become
enforceable in a separatist territory protected by Russian troops.

Television calls this weakness for two days. The file survives those two days
and acquires records, named authorities and a route that does not require the
government to pretend a red arrow on a map is an arrest team.

- @poland_event_queue: Send the address through the legal channels

@romanowski_transnistria_conduct
title: Support safe conduct only with strict appearance and non-obstruction terms
subtitle: Try to exchange pre-trial freedom for a real courtroom; lose polling ground to everyone who hears immunity rather than procedure.
on-arrival: constitutional_restraint += 4; judicial_legitimacy += 3; justice_fund_salience += 6; public_trust -= 1; left_poll_momentum -= 0.2; pis_relation += 2; public_mood_pending_rule_of_law_support += 2; public_mood_pending_rule_of_law_backlash += 4; news_headline = "Lewica accepts a tightly conditioned safe-conduct hearing for Romanowski"

Safe conduct exists to bring a person abroad before a court without pre-trial
detention deciding the case first. Lewica supports a hearing only if every
appearance date, travel restriction and consequence of obstruction is written
into the order.

It is defensible procedure and dreadful television. The phrase **remain free**
runs without the conditions beneath pictures of Tyraspol monuments. A courtroom
may gain a defendant; the party pays before anyone learns whether he boards the
return flight.

- @poland_event_queue: Put the conditions before the court

@romanowski_transnistria_show
title: Put Romanowski and Ziobro on the same departures board
subtitle: The tastiest poll swing: turn two legal files into one prime-time travel serial and accept the institutional hangover.
on-arrival: media_capacity += 5; left_poll_momentum += 0.65; pis_poll_momentum -= 0.75; konf_poll_momentum += 0.1; justice_fund_salience -= 3; public_trust -= 1; institutional_trust -= 3; public_mood_pending_rule_of_law_backlash += 7; public_mood_pending_rule_of_law_left_ownership += 3; news_headline = "The Justice Ministry departures board adds Tyraspol in permanent marker"

The graphic is irresistible. Ziobro's arrows occupy one side, Romanowski's
Budapest–Tyraspol line the other, and the caption reads **two former ministers,
one fund, no convenient jurisdiction**. Every update opens with train music
and closes with the amount intended for crime victims.

The clips do exactly what they were built to do. PiS spends a week explaining
why its patriots keep choosing governments aligned with Moscow when Polish
courts ask questions. The Justice Fund becomes a travel show, the evidence
becomes set dressing and the polling reward arrives much earlier than any
verdict.

- @poland_event_queue: Roll the departures board
